Here are the three TUnit patterns:

**1. Skip a single test method**

```csharp
[Test]
[Skip("Waiting on payment-gateway sandbox provisioning")]
public async Task ChargeCard_ReturnsSuccess()
{
    // ...
}
```

**2. Skip every test in an assembly (conditionally)**

In a top-level file (e.g. `AssemblyAttributes.cs`):

```csharp
#if SMOKE
[assembly: Skip("Assembly skipped: smoke configuration — full suite not intended")]
#endif
```

Build with `-c Smoke` and define the `SMOKE` constant in your `.csproj`:

```xml
<PropertyGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Smoke'">
  <DefineConstants>SMOKE</DefineConstants>
</PropertyGroup>
```

**3. Runtime skip inside a test method**

```csharp
[Test]
public async Task SyncAdUsers_PullsFullDirectory()
{
    if (!IsOnCorporateVpn())
        Skip.Test("Machine is not on the corporate VPN — skipping AD sync test");

    // ... rest of test
}

private static bool IsOnCorporateVpn()
{
    // e.g. try to resolve an internal DNS name
    try
    {
        Dns.GetHostEntry("internal.corp.example.com");
        return true;
    }
    catch { return false; }
}
```

**Summary**

| Scope | Mechanism | Runs any test code? |
|---|---|---|
| Single method | `[Skip("reason")]` on the method | No |
| Whole assembly | `[assembly: Skip("reason")]` | No |
| Runtime decision | `Skip.Test("reason")` inside the body | Yes, up to the skip call |

All three report the reason string in the test output so CI logs clearly show *why* tests were skipped.
